    "items": [
      {
        "question": "Is All Natural teeth cleaning a replacement for care provided by my veterinarian?",
        "answer": "No. Our ALL NATURAL teeth cleaning service is designed to complement your pet’s regular care, not replace it. We focus specifically on gently removing visible tartar and plaque buildup.",
        "className": ""
      },
      {
        "className": "",
        "question": "What happens if my pet tries to bite during their appointment?",
        "answer": "Your pet’s safety and our safety are both very important to us. We carefully observe your pet’s body language. If we feel that continuing would not be comfortable or appropriate for your pet, we will stop the cleaning."
      },
      {
        "className": "",
        "answer": "Yes. For pets who benefit from more frequent maintenance cleans, we offer a lower rate for visits scheduled less than six months apart.",
        "question": "Is there a lower price for more frequent maintenance visits?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "No. Our pricing is based on the type and frequency of the visit, not the size of your pet. A tiny dog can be just as wiggly as a large dog!",
        "question": "Does the size of my pet affect the price?",
        "className": ""
      },
      {
        "className": "",
        "answer": "Payment is made after your pet’s cleaning is complete. We also offer our No-Charge Guarantee — if we are unable to clean your pet’s teeth, there is no charge for the visit.",
        "question": "Do I pay before or after the cleaning?"
      },
      {
        "className": "",
        "answer": "We generally prefer to work with your pet one-on-one. Many pets settle more easily without the distraction of their owner nearby. However, if having you nearby would help, we will contact you.",
        "question": "Can I stay with my pet during the cleaning?"
      },
      {
        "className": "",
        "answer": "We have experience working with nervous pets, seniors and rescue animals. We take things slowly, introduce our tools gradually, and allow them time to settle in. There is never a need to rush.",
        "question": "How do you work with nervous or anxious pets?"
      }
    ],
